{
  "id": "grammar.a1.117.b",
  "title": "This one or the one you know",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Pointing at a dish",
      "content": "When you point at a dish on the menu, use *ezt* or *azt* and the definite verb: *Ezt kérem.* (I'll have this one.) The same goes for a dish you both know, with *a*: *Kérem a halat.* (I'll have the fish.) The waiter or a friend asks the same way: *Kéred a halat?* (Do you want the fish?)"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Indefinite and definite",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Kérek egy halat.",
          "I'd like a fish. (any)"
        ],
        [
          "Kérem a halat.",
          "I'll have the fish. (that one)"
        ],
        [
          "Kéred a levest?",
          "Do you want the soup?"
        ],
        [
          "Anna a tésztát kéri.",
          "Anna has the pasta."
        ],
        [
          "Ezt kérem, azt nem.",
          "I'll have this, not that."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "Examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "Ezt kérem.",
          "english": "I'll have this one."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Azt nem kérem.",
          "english": "I won't have that one."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Kéred a halat?",
          "english": "Do you want the fish?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Igen, kérem.",
          "english": "Yes, I'll have it."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Anna a tésztát kéri.",
          "english": "Anna has the pasta."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Choose by the dish, as at the market: *egy* or no article means *kérek*; *a*, *az*, *ezt* or *azt* means *kérem* (*kéred*, *kéri*)."
    }
  ]
}
